== Solution 7 (Pythag bash with same diagram as Solution 6) ==
We start by making the right triangle as in Solution 6.
<imath>AG = (15 - g)</imath>
Since <imath>OE</imath> is a radius to a tangent line, <imath>\angle OEA = \angle OED = 90^{\circ}</imath>. 
Drawing <imath>OD</imath> and using the Pythagorean Theorem on triangle <imath>OCD</imath> gives <imath>OD = \sqrt{g^2 + 400}</imath>. 
Using the Pythagorean Theorem on triangle <imath>OED</imath> gives <imath>ED = \sqrt{g^2 + 300}</imath>.
Using the Pythaogrean Theorem on triangle <imath>OBA</imath> gives <imath>OA = 25</imath>
Using the Pythagorean Theorem on triangle <imath>OEA</imath> gives <imath>EA = 5\sqrt{21}</imath>.

Since <imath>BCDG</imath> is a rectangle, <imath>BC = GD = 40</imath>.

Using the Pythagorean Theorem on triangle <imath>AGD</imath> gives <imath>(15-g)^2 + 40^2 = (5\sqrt{21} + \sqrt{g^2 + 300})^2</imath>
Expanding the squares, rearranging, and simplifying gives the quadratic <imath>3g^2 + 150g - 925 = 0</imath>.
Completing the square and taking only the positive square root seems like the easiest method to find <imath>g</imath> here
<cmath>3(g^2 + 50g) = 925</cmath>
<cmath>3(g^2 + 50g + 625) = 925 + 3(625)</cmath>
<cmath>(g+25) = \sqrt{\frac{2800}{3}} = \frac{20\sqrt{21}}{3}</cmath>
<cmath>g = \frac{20\sqrt{21} - 75}{3}</cmath>

Finally, <imath>a + b + c + d = 20 + 21 + 75 + 3 = \boxed{\textbf{(A) 119}}</imath>

